
Health probes act as Kubernetes' eyes and ears inside your running containers. Without probes, Kubernetes only knows if the container's PID 1 process has terminated. If a process enters an infinite loop, memory leak deadlock, or connection pool exhaustion, the process remains technically "running," yet the application is entirely broken.

Deep Dive: The Three Probe Types

 Probe Type   |              The Question It Asks             |                When It Executes               | Action on Failure                                                                               | Typical Real-World Use Case                                                      |
|:--------------:|:---------------------------------------------:|:---------------------------------------------:|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| startupProbe   | "Has the app initialized yet?"                | Only during initial container launch.         | Kills container and restarts it (restartPolicy). Disables liveness & readiness until it passes. | Heavy legacy apps, JVM/Spring Boot warmup, running DB schema migrations on boot. |
| readinessProbe | "Can the app take user traffic right now?"    | Periodically throughout the entire lifecycle. | Cuts network traffic by detaching Pod IP from EndpointSlice. No restart.                        | App is temporarily overloaded, warming up internal cache, or lost DB connection. |
| livenessProbe  | "Is the app stuck in an unrecoverable state?" | Periodically after startup succeeds.          | Restarts container (kubelet terminates and re-spawns the container process).                    | Catching deadlocks, frozen event loops, or unhandled fatal thread exceptions.    |


