Production Probe Traps
Cascading Failures: Do not configure a livenessProbe to ping a shared downstream database. If the database experiences high latency, every application container's liveness check will time out simultaneously, causing a cluster-wide restart loop. Use readinessProbe for downstream checks instead.

Aggressive Timeouts: Always provide an adequate initialDelaySeconds or leverage a dedicated startupProbe to avoid premature pod kills during initialization.



How Probes Check Application Health

httpGet: Sends an HTTP GET request. Any response status ≥200 and <400 is marked as success.

readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /ready
    port: 8080

tcpSocket: Attempts a TCP handshake on a specific port. Useful for non-HTTP services like databases, Redis, or gRPC endpoints.

livenessProbe:
  tcpSocket:
    port: 5432

exec: Runs an arbitrary command inside the container namespace. Return code 0 is success; non-zero is failure.

livenessProbe:
  exec:
    command:
    - cat
    - /tmp/healthy

Tuning Parameters & The "Deadly Cascading Failure" Trap
Misconfiguring probes is one of the most common causes of production outages:

initialDelaySeconds: How long to wait before firing the first check.

periodSeconds: How frequently to poll the container (e.g., every 5s or 10s).

timeoutSeconds: Seconds before considering a non-responsive check a timeout failure.

failureThreshold: Number of consecutive failures required before taking action.

Production Pitfall: Never tie a livenessProbe directly to an external database call. If your database becomes slow under peak load, your database queries will time out. A naive livenessProbe checking that database query will fail, causing Kubernetes to simultaneously kill and restart all your application pods. The restarted pods will hammer the database with new connection pools upon boot, causing a total cluster-wide blackout.

Rule of thumb: Keep livenessProbe checking only internal memory/deadlock state, and let readinessProbe handle temporary downstream dependency failures.


